@startuml
' Question: Where do the runtime components live, and what crosses
' which environment / network / trust boundary?
'
' Audience: <technology architects, SREs, security review>
'
' Replace nodes and components with real artifact IDs from your
' technology and environment templates.

title <Subject> deployment

skinparam rectangle {
    BackgroundColor #f9fafb
    BorderColor #374151
}

cloud "Internet" as Internet {
}

node "<Environment 1: production, region eu-west-1>\nENV-XXXX" as EnvProd {
    rectangle "<App container 1>\nAPP-XXXX" as App1
    rectangle "<App container 2>\nAPP-XXXX" as App2
    database "<Primary datastore>\nTC-XXXX" as DB
    queue "<Event broker>\nTC-XXXX" as Bus
}

node "<Environment 2: production, region eu-west-2 (DR)>\nENV-XXXX" as EnvDR {
    rectangle "<App container 1 (standby)>\nAPP-XXXX" as App1DR
    database "<Replica datastore>\nTC-XXXX" as DBDR
}

cloud "External SaaS" as ExtSaaS {
    rectangle "<External system>\nAPP-XXXX" as Ext
}

Internet --> App1 : HTTPS\ningress controller
App1 --> DB : reads / writes
App1 --> Bus : publishes
App2 --> Bus : consumes
DB --> DBDR : async replication
App1 --> Ext : API call\noutbound only

note bottom of EnvDR
    Standby region; RTO <subject>, RPO <subject>.
    Failover procedure in <transition-architecture id> /
    runbook reference.
end note

' Related Artifacts:
' - ENV-XXXX (each environment shown)
' - APP-XXXX (each application or container)
' - TC-XXXX (each datastore, queue, runtime, platform)
' - IF-XXXX (interfaces crossing trust boundaries)
' - TS-XXXX (technology standards governing the placements)
@enduml
